WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.453
- [Narrator] We'd like to thank
NordPass for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.730
The American dream,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.098
it's the idea that in the United States,

00:00:09.098 --> 00:00:12.387
upward mobility is available to everyone.

00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:14.360
We're told from an early age

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.298
that if you work hard and sacrifice,

00:00:16.298 --> 00:00:19.574
one day you too, will
have a beautiful family,

00:00:19.574 --> 00:00:23.310
money in the bank and of
course your very own house

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.980
with a white picket fence.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.340
- Today, we don't really appreciate

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.246
how radical this idea is.

00:00:29.246 --> 00:00:32.910
For most of human history,
real estate was where

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.930
money and power were wholly concentrated

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.850
and only the most connected
and wealthy families

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.270
could be landowners.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.890
- Since the 19th century,
millions of people in our country

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.939
really did climb the
socioeconomic ladder this way.

00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:49.890
The creation of a burgeoning middle-class

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.690
was a remarkable achievement
and a revolutionary step

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.280
towards a society in
which wealth is shared

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.718
more evenly amongst its citizens.

00:00:58.718 --> 00:01:01.786
But the ugly truth is that for decades,

00:01:01.786 --> 00:01:05.350
the federal government
intentionally created programs

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.710
that ensured the dream
only became a reality

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.950
for one type of person.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.448
And to this day, we are
dealing with its consequences.

00:01:14.448 --> 00:01:17.031
(gentle music)

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.870
- Back in the 1930s,
our country was reeling

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.400
from years of economic depression
and housing was in crisis.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.720
Many banks had failed,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.000
causing them to call their loans early,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.890
contributing to a wave of foreclosures.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.820
Home ownership across the
nation hovered around 45%.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.080
And the housing inventory
that did remain, was tight.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.250
Especially for those in the
middle and lower classes.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.953
- In response, Congress passed

00:01:48.953 --> 00:01:52.120
the National Housing Act in 1934,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.230
which created the Federal
Housing Administration.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.180
The FHA's job was to make buying a house

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.640
affordable to the everyday person,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.100
mainly by offering to
insure certain home loans

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.330
against borrower default.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.040
This government sponsored safety net

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.373
gave private banks and
lenders the confidence

00:02:11.373 --> 00:02:14.240
to extend much more generous terms

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.350
than had ever been available in the past.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.010
Like 30 year mortgages,
higher lending limits

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.374
and low fixed rates.

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:24.011
The sort of things we
take for granted today

00:02:24.011 --> 00:02:26.626
and it was a resounding success.

00:02:26.626 --> 00:02:30.520
The FHA helped take the
country's home ownership rate

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.520
from 43.6% to just under 70% by 2005.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:38.480
Heck, we even got an FHA loan

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.621
when we bought our house back in 2010.

00:02:40.621 --> 00:02:42.100
Thanks FHA.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.110
- But there's a very big difference

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.650
between the original FHA mortgages

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.030
and the ones we have access to today.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.720
Nowadays, approval for an FHA mortgage

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.350
is based on your personal
financial situation

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.810
and credit worthiness.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.300
But originally, FHA mortgages
focused on some other factors.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.280
Let's crack open the 1936
FHA Underwriting Manual.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.170
Shall we?

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:10.120
It stated, if a neighborhood
is to retain stability,

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.000
it is necessary that
properties be occupied

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.700
but the same social and racial classes.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.000
Schools should not be
attended in large numbers

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.300
by inharmonious racial groups.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.490
If the children living in such an area

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.380
are compelled to attend schools

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.020
where the majority of the peoples

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.090
represent a far lower level of society

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.410
or an incompatible racial element,

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.760
the neighborhood will prove
far less stable and desirable.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.510
- So, to quote the FHA,
communities of color

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:43.940
were branded as undesirable populations

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.770
and their mere presence would
have a detrimental influence

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.124
on the housing prices surrounding them.

00:03:50.124 --> 00:03:54.350
But this stance was not
supported by any evidence.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.710
In reality, when people of
color managed to buy homes

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:59.520
in mostly white neighborhoods,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.680
the property values would go up

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.430
because they were willing to pay more

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.640
due to the shrinking housing
inventory available to them.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.880
- Nevertheless, cities across the nation

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.730
ended up being divided into
specific zones by the FHA

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.670
and local housing authorities.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.630
The areas where whites
were more concentrated

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.607
got the green light for
those sweet FHA backed loans.

00:04:21.607 --> 00:04:24.560
But the areas with higher concentrations

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.310
of communities of color and
immigrants were sectioned off

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:30.270
on maps with red lines

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.750
to indicate they were not
eligible for FHA loans

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.210
regardless of individual
financial credentials.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.668
This practice, now
referred to as red lining,

00:04:40.668 --> 00:04:43.690
also occurred for new
affordable developments

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.440
popping up in suburbs.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.468
Many of those builders even
added language to deeds,

00:04:48.468 --> 00:04:50.710
known as restrictive covenants,

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:54.240
with phrasing like no
property shall be sold,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.788
conveyed, rented or leased to any person

00:04:56.788 --> 00:04:59.555
not of the white or Caucasian race.

00:04:59.555 --> 00:05:02.456
Yep, it was that explicit.

00:05:02.456 --> 00:05:05.580
- The combination of federal policy

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.850
and private sector reinforcement

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:09.930
was devastatingly effective.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.930
Between 1934 and 1968,
black families specifically,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:19.744
received only 2% of all
federally insured, home loans.

00:05:19.744 --> 00:05:22.940
And housing segregation tightened its grip

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.700
on the American landscape.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.660
It wasn't until 1968 that title eight

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.340
of the Fair Housing Act finally outlawed

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.100
housing discrimination.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.430
Think about that.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:37.672
For 34 years, an entire
generation of people

00:05:37.672 --> 00:05:41.930
denied access to one of the
biggest tools of wealth building

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.755
of the entire 20th century.

00:05:43.755 --> 00:05:46.880
It's depressingly easy to find evidence

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.330
of its impact all these years later.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.828
- According to the federal reserve,

00:05:50.828 --> 00:05:53.220
the typical white family today,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.670
has eight times the wealth
of a typical black family.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.747
And five times the wealth of
the typical Hispanic family.

00:05:59.747 --> 00:06:04.160
This is in large part, due to
home equity being bequeathed

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.942
from one generation to the next.

00:06:05.942 --> 00:06:08.520
Nearly 30% of white families

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.200
report receiving an
inheritance or monetary gift

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.680
from family compared to
10% of black families

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.330
and 7% of Hispanic families.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.010
- And even though it's been 52 years

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.432
since those FHA policies
were ruled unconstitutional,

00:06:23.432 --> 00:06:25.910
the gap between white home ownership

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.139
and black home ownership is
the worst it's ever been.

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:32.410
And even if a black
family does own a home,

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.870
the typical value of it is only 65%

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:37.770
of the average white families.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.050
We even see this sort of divide play out

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.955
in the most elite circles.

00:06:41.955 --> 00:06:46.115
Of the 616 billionaires
that call America home,

00:06:46.115 --> 00:06:49.280
only seven are black and none of them

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.937
the product of inheritance.

00:06:50.937 --> 00:06:54.060
- And honestly, the deeper
you go on this subject,

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.140
the more it feels like
you're peeling back layers

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.429
upon layers of rotted,
termite infested wood.

00:07:00.429 --> 00:07:03.730
Housing policy directly impacts education

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:07.150
because we fund our public
schools via property taxes.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.890
Lower property values means
less money for schools

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.990
resulting in lower graduation rates,

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.817
lower college attendance, et cetera.

00:07:13.817 --> 00:07:17.880
Then there's the impact on
policing, incarceration rates,

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.345
mental health, even our diet.

00:07:20.345 --> 00:07:22.920
Areas with higher concentrations of black,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.850
Latino and indigenous Americans

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:26.921
are far more likely to lack easy access

00:07:26.921 --> 00:07:29.230
to fresh and affordable food,

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.511
creating what are known as food deserts.

00:07:31.511 --> 00:07:33.770
This leaves the residents of these areas

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:36.430
more susceptible to chronic health issues.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.150
- We here at Two Cents, deeply believe

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.700
that taking ownership of your finances

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.147
can completely change the game.

00:07:44.147 --> 00:07:47.270
But we collectively have to acknowledge

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.420
that the cards are not fairly dealt.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:52.869
We are perfect examples of this.

00:07:52.869 --> 00:07:56.590
We take our financial
responsibilities very seriously

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.879
through budgeting, saving,
and living within our means,

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:03.350
but we are fourth generation homeowners.

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:06.430
And when we bought our FHA
insured house a decade ago,

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:10.081
we were graciously gifted
some of our down-payment.

00:08:10.081 --> 00:08:13.149
Our net worth, like everyone else's,

00:08:13.149 --> 00:08:18.149
is due to a combination of
choice and circumstance.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:21.510
- We also hope this show helps strip away

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.120
the taboo from money talk.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:25.591
How can we find solutions to issues

00:08:25.591 --> 00:08:27.300
that we aren't educated on

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.700
and don't converse openly about?

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.020
This subject is no different.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.341
As the writer, At-Nehisi Coates puts it,

00:08:34.341 --> 00:08:36.993
All of our solutions to the great problems

00:08:36.993 --> 00:08:41.479
of healthcare, education,
housing and economic inequality

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:45.490
are troubled by what must go unspoken.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:48.300
- Hopefully, if enough of us honestly talk

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:49.712
about this rigged game,

00:08:49.712 --> 00:08:53.820
we just might be able to see
more people actually win.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.200
And that's our Two Cents.
- And that's our Two Cents.

