00:09:45.485 --> 00:09:49.080
- Hey everybody, we are back to answer

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.280
some of your questions and
reply to some of your comments

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.298
on our last episode, which was

00:09:54.298 --> 00:09:58.060
should the U.S. government
balance its budget?

00:09:58.060 --> 00:09:59.490
- All right, our first comment
that we're gonna address

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.910
is from John L., he says,
"all my life I heard

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.023
that the U.S. government
could print money if needed,

00:10:05.023 --> 00:10:07.807
I just thought it was an
old wives tale, until now.

00:10:07.807 --> 00:10:09.890
Am I understanding this correctly?

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:11.291
If they find themselves in hot water

00:10:11.291 --> 00:10:13.510
that they can just print some more money?"

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.190
Also, is the U.S. the only
country that can do this

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:20.850
or others can as well but we
never hear about this ability?

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.133
- What John is alluding to here,

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:28.030
is what economists now call
modern monetary theory.

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.670
It's just a theory, but
it's kind of this idea

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.940
of maybe we should start thinking about

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:35.890
the government printing
more money differently.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:37.770
So to this first part of it,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:40.960
can they just kind of make more money

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.520
to get out of hot water?

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.490
No, not necessarily
because making more money,

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:48.490
especially to a higher degree,
does devalue the money.

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.310
And so you can't just do this infinitely

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:51.950
or every time you get into trouble.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.321
- And I actually don't know the answer

00:10:53.321 --> 00:10:55.790
to that ending question, which is,

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.840
is the U.S. the only
country that can do this?

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.949
I'm assuming not, right?

00:10:59.949 --> 00:11:01.000
What do you know about this?

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.050
- So not every country can do this.

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:07.464
You have to have a sovereign
currency for this to work.

00:11:07.464 --> 00:11:10.450
So because the U.S. has its own currency,

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:12.270
the dollar, we can do this

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.910
because we fully control our own currency.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.823
But the Euro zone that has
a lot of different countries

00:11:17.823 --> 00:11:20.520
with the same currency, the Euro,

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.699
cannot do this in the same way

00:11:22.699 --> 00:11:26.067
because they can't just print
more euros in one country.

00:11:26.067 --> 00:11:27.990
It's sort of a different system.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.019
- So, our second question
comes from Robert Murray.

00:11:30.019 --> 00:11:32.071
Do you two have any strong opinions

00:11:32.071 --> 00:11:34.550
about modern money theory?

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:38.273
Like what Stephanie Kelton and
L. Randall Wray talk about?

00:11:38.273 --> 00:11:41.240
Again, this is a theory,

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.440
so it can only be proven through practice,

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:48.777
but we have a pretty good history

00:11:48.777 --> 00:11:53.550
that seems to prove this to be the case.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.991
- I just listened to an
amazing podcast on this

00:11:55.991 --> 00:11:57.410
from Planet Money.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:11:59.360
It was just called modern monetary theory.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:00.880
They interview Stephanie Kelton,

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.250
who is kind of one of the
forefront thought leaders

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.420
around monetary theory
and hearing them talk

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:09.570
through these ideas and a lot
of the old ways of thinking

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.130
that we kind of talked
about in this episode,

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.850
which is got to balance our budget,

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.481
we can't go into infinite amounts of debt.

00:12:15.481 --> 00:12:18.370
That has kind of proven not to be true.

